::ENG3_U03_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A leader who loves praise more than truth will soon hear only echoes. The honest advisor may trouble the room, but counsel that troubles pride can protect the city. What is the central idea? {
=Wise leaders need honest counsel more than praise.#Correct. The passage contrasts praise with truthful advice.
~Leaders should avoid all advisors.#The passage values honest advisors.
~Praise is always the same as truth.#The passage contrasts praise and truth.
~Cities are protected by echoes.#Echoes symbolize empty praise, not protection.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A leader who loves praise more than truth will soon hear only echoes. The honest advisor may trouble the room, but counsel that troubles pride can protect the city. Which detail supports the central idea? {
~"loves praise"#This introduces the problem but is not the full support.
="counsel that troubles pride can protect the city"#Correct. This detail shows why honest counsel matters.
~"only echoes"#This image supports the problem but not as directly as the counsel detail.
~"the room"#This is setting language, not the main support.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A leader who loves praise more than truth will soon hear only echoes. The honest advisor may trouble the room, but counsel that troubles pride can protect the city. What is the topic? {
~Farming and harvest.#Not discussed.
~Ship repair.#Not discussed.
=Leadership and counsel.#Correct. The passage focuses on leaders and honest advice.
~Music in public rooms.#Echoes are figurative, not the topic.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A leader who loves praise more than truth will soon hear only echoes. The honest advisor may trouble the room, but counsel that troubles pride can protect the city. How does the structure develop the idea? {
~It defines every word before making a claim.#The passage does not use definitions.
~It lists unrelated examples.#The contrast is related.
~It narrates one advisor's full life.#No life story appears.
=It contrasts empty praise with difficult but useful counsel.#Correct. The contrast develops the central idea.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Passage: A leader who loves praise more than truth will soon hear only echoes. The honest advisor may trouble the room, but counsel that troubles pride can protect the city. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~Leaders should only listen to people who praise them.#This contradicts the passage.
=Leaders protect the city when they accept truthful advice, even when it challenges their pride.#Correct. This accurately restates the idea.
~Advisors should never speak in public rooms.#Unsupported.
~Truth is dangerous because it protects no one.#The passage says counsel can protect the city.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: Wealth kept in a locked chest feeds no neighbor and teaches no child. Wealth used wisely becomes a road, a well, or a school. Possession is small; stewardship is large. What is the central idea? {
~All wealth should remain locked away.#The passage criticizes locked wealth.
~Roads, wells, and schools are useless.#The passage presents them as wise uses.
=Resources matter most when used for the public good.#Correct. The passage values stewardship over possession.
~Neighbors should avoid schools.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: Wealth kept in a locked chest feeds no neighbor and teaches no child. Wealth used wisely becomes a road, a well, or a school. Possession is small; stewardship is large. Which detail best supports the central idea? {
~"locked chest"#This supports the contrast but not the wise use directly.
~"Possession is small"#This is part of the conclusion.
~"no neighbor"#This shows what locked wealth fails to do.
="becomes a road, a well, or a school"#Correct. These examples show public benefit.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: Wealth kept in a locked chest feeds no neighbor and teaches no child. Wealth used wisely becomes a road, a well, or a school. Possession is small; stewardship is large. What is the topic? {
=The responsible use of wealth.#Correct. The passage is about wealth used for public benefit.
~The design of chests.#The chest is an image, not the topic.
~The history of roads only.#Roads are one example.
~The weather near a school.#Not discussed.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: Wealth kept in a locked chest feeds no neighbor and teaches no child. Wealth used wisely becomes a road, a well, or a school. Possession is small; stewardship is large. What structure develops the central idea? {
~Chronological sequence from morning to evening.#No time sequence appears.
=Contrast between possession and stewardship.#Correct. The structure contrasts selfish possession with public use.
~Question and answer.#No question is asked.
~A list of unrelated objects only.#The objects support stewardship.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: Wealth kept in a locked chest feeds no neighbor and teaches no child. Wealth used wisely becomes a road, a well, or a school. Possession is small; stewardship is large. Which answer is only a topic, not a central idea? {
~Resources should serve public good.#This is a central idea.
~Stewardship is greater than possession.#This is a central idea.
=Wealth.#Correct. "Wealth" is a subject, not a complete central idea.
~Private possession is less valuable than wise public use.#This is a central idea.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: The brave person is not the one who never trembles. The brave person measures the danger, remembers the duty, and steps forward because another life depends on it. What is the central idea? {
~Bravery means feeling no fear.#The passage says the brave person may tremble.
=True courage means acting from duty despite fear.#Correct. This idea is developed throughout the passage.
~Danger should always be ignored.#The brave person measures danger.
~Duty has no connection to courage.#Duty is central to the passage.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: The brave person is not the one who never trembles. The brave person measures the danger, remembers the duty, and steps forward because another life depends on it. Which detail supports the central idea? {
~"never trembles"#This is what the passage rejects.
~"The brave person"#This names the subject.
~"danger"#This is only one part of the idea.
="remembers the duty, and steps forward"#Correct. This shows action despite danger.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Passage: The brave person is not the one who never trembles. The brave person measures the danger, remembers the duty, and steps forward because another life depends on it. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~Courage requires not understanding danger.#The passage says the brave person measures danger.
~A brave person should never help another life.#This contradicts the passage.
=A brave person may feel fear but acts responsibly when someone needs help.#Correct. This accurately restates the central idea.
~Trembling proves a person cannot be brave.#The passage rejects that idea.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: The brave person is not the one who never trembles. The brave person measures the danger, remembers the duty, and steps forward because another life depends on it. How does the passage develop the central idea? {
~It compares bravery to wealth.#Wealth is not discussed.
~It gives a recipe for travel.#No travel recipe appears.
~It hides the main idea in unrelated facts.#The details are directly related.
=It first rejects a false definition of bravery, then gives a more complete definition.#Correct. This structure develops the central idea clearly.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A law written on stone cannot make people just if their habits remain crooked. Justice begins in repeated choices: a fair weight, an honest witness, a patient hearing. What is the central idea? {
=Justice depends on everyday habits, not only written laws.#Correct. The passage contrasts law on stone with repeated choices.
~Stone laws are always enough.#The passage says they are not enough.
~Justice begins only in courts.#The examples include daily choices.
~Habits do not matter.#The passage says habits matter.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A law written on stone cannot make people just if their habits remain crooked. Justice begins in repeated choices: a fair weight, an honest witness, a patient hearing. Which detail supports the central idea? {
~"written on stone"#This shows written law, but the main support is in the examples of choices.
="a fair weight, an honest witness, a patient hearing"#Correct. These examples show daily habits of justice.
~"crooked"#This describes bad habits but not the positive examples.
~"law"#This is topic-related but not enough alone.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: A law written on stone cannot make people just if their habits remain crooked. Justice begins in repeated choices: a fair weight, an honest witness, a patient hearing. What structure helps develop the central idea? {
=A contrast between written law and lived habits.#Correct. This contrast develops the central idea.
~A chronological sequence of a single day.#No time sequence appears.
~A comparison of two cities.#No two cities are compared.
~A definition of stone.#Stone is not being defined.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A law written on stone cannot make people just if their habits remain crooked. Justice begins in repeated choices: a fair weight, an honest witness, a patient hearing. Which statement confuses topic with central idea? {
~Justice requires fair habits.#This is a central idea.
~Written law alone cannot create justice.#This is a central idea.
=Justice.#Correct. This is only a topic.
~Daily choices develop justice.#This is a central idea.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: The speaker says, "A council that hears only the wealthy rules with one ear closed." What central idea does this detail support? {
~Councils should avoid listening.#The detail criticizes limited listening.
=Good government must listen to all groups, not only the powerful.#Correct. The image of one ear closed supports inclusive counsel.
~Wealthy citizens should never speak.#The passage says "only the wealthy" is the problem.
~Ears are the topic of government.#The ear image is figurative.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: The speaker says, "A council that hears only the wealthy rules with one ear closed." Which phrase is the strongest evidence for limited perspective? {
~"A council"#This names the governing group.
~"rules"#This names the action.
~"the wealthy"#This names one group, but not the limitation by itself.
="only the wealthy"#Correct. "Only" shows the council listens to a limited group.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A good law is like a straight road: it should guide the many without bending for the powerful few. What is the central idea? {
~Roads are more important than laws.#The road is a comparison.
=Fair laws should guide everyone equally.#Correct. The passage says law should not bend for the powerful few.
~Powerful people should receive special roads.#This contradicts the passage.
~Good laws should be confusing.#The straight road suggests clarity.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Passage: A good law is like a straight road: it should guide the many without bending for the powerful few. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~A law should help powerful people avoid rules.#This contradicts the passage.
~Roads and laws are exactly the same object.#The road is a comparison.
=A fair law should apply clearly to everyone and not favor powerful people.#Correct. This restates the central idea accurately.
~The passage is mainly about travel plans.#The road supports an idea about law.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: A good law is like a straight road: it should guide the many without bending for the powerful few. What feature of the sentence helps develop the idea? {
~A random list of unrelated facts.#No list appears.
~A question with no answer.#No question appears.
~A time sequence.#No sequence appears.
=A comparison between law and a straight road.#Correct. The comparison clarifies fairness and consistency.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: The essay argues that friendship is proven not at feasts but in hardship, when comfort must be shared and pride must be set aside. What is the central idea? {
=True friendship is shown through support during difficulty.#Correct. The passage contrasts feasts with hardship.
~Friendship only exists at feasts.#The passage says friendship is proven in hardship.
~Pride is the same as friendship.#The passage says pride must be set aside.
~Comfort should never be shared.#The passage says comfort must be shared.
}

::ENG3_U03_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: The essay argues that friendship is proven not at feasts but in hardship, when comfort must be shared and pride must be set aside. Which detail supports the central idea? {
="when comfort must be shared and pride must be set aside"#Correct. This detail explains what hardship requires of friendship.
~"feasts"#This is the contrast, not the strongest support.
~"the essay argues"#This names the text type.
~"not at feasts"#This begins the contrast.
}
